Top 10 issues identified in src/wids/:

1. Overuse of assert for runtime errors
   Functions like splitname, MD5‐sum checks, and spec loading use assert, which is stripped with -O. Use explicit exceptions instead.

2. Resource leaks & unclosed handles
   hash_localname never closes its SQLite connection; MMIndexedTar and download_and_open can leave streams or mmap handles open, leading to FD leaks.

3. keep flag in LRUShards is a no-op
   The constructor accepts keep=False but never uses it; evicted shards are always closed.

4. LRUCache & open_objects not thread-safe
   Neither the in-memory LRU nor the global open_objects dict use locks, risking races in multi-worker DataLoader contexts.

5. Brittle, external-tool-only downloads
   HTTP/S/FTP/S3/GS handlers shell out to curl/aws/gsutil with no fallback or availability check, causing abrupt failures if CLIs are missing.

6. Manual tar parsing is fragile
   MMIndexedTar re-implements tar parsing without PAX or GNU extension support; TarFileReader duplicates tarfile logic and relies on unstable fileobj offsets.

7. Verbosity gating is broken
   Modules print unconditionally or use flawed `if True or os.getenv("WIDS_VERBOSE")`: you can’t silence unwanted logs.

8. Confusing key naming (leading dot)
   Sample dict keys start with a dot (e.g. ".jpg"), forcing all users to remember and handle that convention.

9. default_decoder signature bug
   default_decoder declares `format: Union[bool,str]=True` then calls `format.lower()`, raising TypeError if called with the default True.

10. Missing dependency on requests
    wids_specs.load_remote_dsdesc_raw falls back to import requests but requests is not in the core dependencies.